# Machine-readable citation metadata.
#
# GitHub renders a "Cite this repository" button from this file and offers
# BibTeX and APA from it. Zenodo reads it when minting a DOI for a tagged
# release, which is why the author identity here is an ORCID rather than an
# email address: `docs/specs/2026-09-14-disclosure-authorship-and-citation.md`
# states the goal as "authorship unambiguous and permanent," and an ORCID
# survives the loss of an email account, a domain, or an institution.
#
# `version` and `date-released` are deliberately ABSENT. Both are facts about
# a release, and no release exists yet. Declaring `version: 0.1.0` here would
# be a second copy of `[product] version` in fiducial.toml — the duplication
# this repository gates everywhere else — and a `date-released` for a release
# that was never tagged is simply false. The tagged-release + Zenodo DOI work
# is what earns those two keys, and it should DERIVE them rather than
# hand-write them.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: Fiducial
abstract: >-
  A platform for single-operator product development in which every fact is
  declared once and every artifact derived from it, across electronics,
  firmware, protocol, web and mechanical domains — with machine-checked
  guarantees that a derived artifact which has drifted from its declaration
  cannot reach the main branch.
type: software

authors:
  - family-names: Zdravković
    given-names: Aleksa
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0004-1535-1839"

repository-code: "https://github.com/AleksaZCodes/fiducial"
url: "https://github.com/AleksaZCodes/fiducial"
license: MIT

keywords:
  - single-source-of-truth
  - code-generation
  - build-systems
  - electronic-design-automation
  - embedded-systems
  - developer-tools
  - reproducibility
